> Ignoring the request would leave the peer waiting for a response that will never arrive, which is undesirable.

Agreed — dropping the suggestion to ignore.

A third option that seems to dominate both disconnect and full-block: reply with an empty `blocktxn`. Master already sends one for any block within the `MAX_BLOCKTXN_DEPTH` window (my baseline test above shows the tip case), and `BlockTransactions{req}` is built entirely from the request, so placed at the top of the handler the reply needs neither the block nor any lookup:

```cpp
if (req.indexes.empty()) {
    MakeAndPushMessage(pfrom, NetMsgType::BLOCKTXN, BlockTransactions{req});
    return;
}
```

And you're right there's a real read to save — below the cached tip but within the window, master reads the block from disk and uses none of it (I'd only looked at the in-memory path earlier). The short-circuit eliminates that read too, at ~40 bytes of response. Full-block forwarding seems weakest on this PR's own motivation: a disk read plus a full block of bandwidth in reply to a request for nothing. (Arguably BIP152 already covers the zero-length case — "exactly and only" the transactions at zero indexes is well-defined — but the proposal doesn't rest on that.)

One note on that placement: it's what makes the reply lookup-free, but it also means we reply for hashes we don't have, where master is currently silent. The response doesn't depend on whether we have the block, so I don't see an issue; if preferred, the check works after `LookupBlockIndex` too, which still skips the disk read though not the lookup itself.
